nation against the reference (feeding G2) or G5's evidence review would still catch the false claim before integration was not traced in this pass.\"\n    root_cause: >\n      `satisfied_by_reference` is read by `preparation.ts` (`~line 116`) to flag a requirement as\n      `undiscriminated` when the reference tree fails to exercise it, feeding a later gate's evidence —\n      but `gateG1` (`~decide.ts:505-515`) never consults it before passing the requirements document, so\n      a false claim is not refused at the gate meant to bind an answer to a real obligation; whether it\n      is refused later depends on control paths this pass did not trace to a verdict.\n    evidence: >\n      Read during the round-1 review of e01s03 (`specs/verifications/e01s03-relecture.md`, P2):\n      `gateG1`'s mandatory-answer loop names no check on `satisfied_by_reference`; `preparation.ts:116-126`\n      is the only reader found, and it is proved only by the tests that already pass, not by a new\n      failing one exercising a false claim. G2 and G5's own handling of the case was not traced.\n    reproduced: >\n      Reproduced on 2026-10-01 with a scripted agent on the reference npm target (`npm run campagne -- npm`).\n      A specification report whose only mandatory requirement asks for a function the tree does not have,\n      marked `satisfied_by_reference: true`, and an implementation that only appends a comment, pass G0 to G5\n      (unit, coverage and mutation PASS, `lastFit` absent from the candidate): `diagnoseControlCapability`\n      reports no undiscriminated requirement while the reference suite executes a test, so no failing test is\n      prepared, no arbitration is asked, and every control stays green on the reference and on the candidate.\n      The same report with `satisfied_by_reference: false` stops at \"no test can judge R1\" and waits for the\n0\n    follow_ups: >\n      Needs a design, not a fix: where the program's global requirements come from and how a sc